program            = 004_va_copy_multiple_passes
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Variadic argument list copied and traversed more than once, including a copy of a copy and a copy ended early, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Header exception: this program includes <stdarg.h>. The corpus sanctions two header
exception classes and only two, and this is the first of them: the programs in area 07 may
include <stdarg.h> and nothing else. The second class is the dedicated bundled-header probe
12_preprocessor/003_bundled_header_inclusion.c, which may include the nine required bundled
freestanding headers; it does not apply here, and no program may take both. The exception is
needed because variadic functions cannot be written at all without
va_list, va_start, va_arg and va_end, and because this program additionally copies a list
with va_copy, which is its whole subject. stdarg.h is in bcc's own bundled
freestanding set, and docs/technical-specifications.md line 208 documents va_copy
explicitly among the macros implemented via compiler builtins, which is precisely what
this program exercises. stdarg.h is also a freestanding header supplied by the reference
compiler, so it compiles identically under both oracles and the program remains a
single-file reproducer. No other header is included; in particular stdio.h is not
included, because bcc ships none. printf is hand-declared.
va_copy lifecycle, the central correctness rule of this program: every va_copy is matched
by its own va_end on the destination list, and each original list is va_end-ed exactly
once. The file contains three va_start invocations, four va_copy invocations and seven
va_end invocations, which is one va_end for each va_start and one for each va_copy. Every
va_end is in the same function as the va_start or va_copy that created its list, as C11
requires. In two_passes the copy is taken before any traversal, then the copy is traversed
fully and ended, then the original is traversed fully and ended. In three_passes the first
copy is taken from the original and the second copy is taken from the first copy, both
before either has been traversed, so all three lists start at the same position; each is
then traversed fully and ended exactly once. In head_tail the copy is read only three
times out of six arguments and then ended without consuming the remainder, which is
explicitly permitted, and the original is read all six times and ended once.
No list is ever read past the number of arguments actually supplied at its call site, so
there is no over-read. Nothing derived from a va_list object itself is ever printed; only
the values retrieved from it are printed.
Retrieval types: int and double arguments are not subject to the default argument
promotions, so va_arg(ap, int) and va_arg(ap, double) retrieve exactly the types supplied.
The floating arguments are written as double literals with no f suffix, so no promotion is
involved and va_arg(ap, float) never appears.
Arithmetic: no signed overflow is possible. The integer operands are small mixed-sign
values and the totals are -6, -10, 6, 15, 21, 60, 150 and 210, all far inside the 32-bit
int range. Floating addition is on dyadic rationals only. No int is ever added to a double
accumulator or the reverse. There is no shift, no division, no aliasing violation, no read
of uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, and no
dependence on padding or on the addresses of unrelated objects.
Evaluation order: each helper prints all of its own derived lines and returns void, so
main makes six plain calls and no call has more than one side-effecting argument. In the
runtime variants each volatile object or volatile array element is written and then read
back into plain storage before the call, so the arguments in the call expression are pure
reads of plain objects and the unspecified argument-evaluation order is unobservable.
Warning gate: this program passes the full default gate, -Wall -Wextra -pedantic
-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror, unmodified. That was verified by
compiling it with exactly that gate on the reference compiler, so this record declares
no deviation and omits the ub_audit_flags key, which is how a record whose program needs
the whole gate says so. Area 07 sanctions no deviation in any case: the only authorized
removals are -pedantic, for the supported-extension area where the subject is
non-standard by definition, and the conversion diagnostics, for the deliberate
narrowing programs where a narrowing conversion is the behaviour under test. Neither
removal applies to a variadic program.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Width normalization: the types long and unsigned long are never used, because sizeof(long)
and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64
(docs/technical-specifications.md lines 457 to 462). Only int and double appear, and the
printf conversions are limited to %s, %d and %.6f. No address, no pointer value, no sizeof
result and no plain char value is printed.
Floating determinism: every floating literal is a dyadic rational exactly representable in
IEEE binary64 (1.5, 2.25, 4.125, 0.5, 3.25, 8.125), and all floating output is printed at
fixed precision with %.6f, so the rendered text is byte-identical on all four backends.
va_list representation differs by target, which is why this program is valuable rather
than why it should be restricted. On i686 a va_list is effectively a single pointer walking
a stack argument block, while on x86-64 System V it is a structure describing a register
save area plus an overflow area (docs/technical-specifications.md lines 546 and 553), and
AArch64 AAPCS64 and RISC-V 64 LP64D use their own register save areas (lines 559 and 565).
va_copy must reproduce whichever representation the target uses, so the semantic invariant
asserted here, that a copied list yields exactly the same values in the same order as the
original, is meaningful on every target and is compared across all four.
Semantic invariants visible in the golden output: each _copy line equals its _orig
counterpart, both _equal lines read 1, each _sums line carries three identical values, and
head_sum plus tail_sum equals total in both split cases.
Cross-backend comparability: integer and double variadic argument passing was measured to
produce byte-identical results on all four targets at all three optimization levels, so no
target is restricted and all three oracles are enabled.
END
